## Project Analysis
This project documents a black-box penetration test conducted against the **Mediroza General Hospital** web application as part of the Network Walks Week 4 cybersecurity lab (Batch B082).

The assessment simulated an authorized penetration test focused on identifying weaknesses that could lead to unauthorized access to confidential patient information and critical organizational data.

### Key Activities

- Conducted web reconnaissance and enumeration against the target application.
- Identified and retrieved three confidential patient laboratory reports.
- Analyzed and cracked weak/predictable PDF passwords using dictionary-based techniques.
- Successfully decrypted protected PDF reports.
- Performed PDF metadata analysis using `exiftool`.
- Identified an internal author reference and a comment revealing the location of a database backup.
- Discovered a publicly accessible `/old/` directory with directory listing enabled.
- Retrieved the exposed `mediroza_db_backup_2019.sql` database backup.
- Analyzed the database and identified sensitive employee salary information.
- Identified shareholder names, ownership percentages, and share classes.
- Correlated the PDF metadata with the staff database to reconstruct the complete attack chain.
- Documented findings, evidence, risks, and remediation recommendations.

### Major Findings

| Finding | Severity |
|---|---|
| Unauthorized exposure of confidential patient reports | 🔴 Critical |
| Weak/predictable PDF password protection | 🟠 High |
| Sensitive information disclosed through PDF metadata | 🟠 High |
| Publicly accessible database backup | 🔴 Critical |
| Exposure of employee salary and shareholder information | 🔴 Critical |

